ing  questions  around open source data science r and  python and our products  great place to get information every  year we sponsor our studio comp  which is the biggest gathering of open  source data science users in the world  we've got our next conference coming up  just in a couple of weeks  if any of you are going to be in the san  francisco area at that time where we  have a couple of slots  available left in that if not we're also  going to be live streaming the  presentations and we'll provide that  link  at the end of the presentation  the our studio education team is devoted  to  helping train the next million open  source data science users  we provide capabilities around not only  pointers to  other learning materials and our own  learning materials  but we also provide a train the trainer  certification  so that we can train um  people in the open source data science  community in uh  teaching on r and really encourage them  to scale out and provide training to  others because we feel like that's the  best way to really  get the capabilities for using our out  across the community  we're also a member of a number of cross  vendor groups  that help support the data science  community we were  one of the founders of the our  consortium which is focuses on  delivering valuable infrastructure and  supporting working groups  for the our community we're now major  sponsors of num focus as well  which is another cross fender group for  supporting investment  in open source data science they're the  umbrella organization that provides the  primary  funding for the jupiter project and  we're helping incubate ursa labs uh  providing operational support and  infrastructure for this industry-funded  development group  which specializes in developing open  source data science tools  that cross languages including most  recently the apache aero project  next slide  and so before we wrap up and head to  questions just a few links  on where you can find more information  to get an overview of what we talked  about today  and links to a lot more um  detailed information your your one stop  uh landing page is rstudio.com  python lots of information there you can  also contact us  to learn more that's a great way if you  want to get a detailed demo  if you want to get some questions  answered or just do a deeper dive  that's the best way to do it we will get  to as many questions as we can today  but there are a ton which is awesome we  appreciate the engagement  if we don't get to your question today  we will follow up  the link to the webinar recording as  well as the slides as well as  the scripts that sean used today all  that will be provided  to the attendees we've gotten several  questions on that  as i mentioned our conference is coming  up in two weeks you can  um click on this link we'll be able to  get more information about it  and uh when once the conference starts  you'll have access to the live stream  um where you'll be able to learn more  after the conference we  post those recordings on our website  those so those will all be available as  well  if you missed the live stream uh  in the first section of his demo sean  focused on using the reticulate package  to call python  from within r and so here's a couple of  links on  the website for the package examples and  a deeper dive webinar for using them  we also got a number of questions around  configuration and versioning and whatnot  and i'll hand a couple of those off to  sean in a minute  but for some of the deeper information  there again  our documentation is a great source for  that we can provide you information  and as always the rstudio community is a  great place  to ask and answers a whole bunch of  questions around  our products and using our python  so with that we'll dive into questions  as i said  if we will do our best to get to all  your questions if we don't we'll follow  up if you'd like to set up a  conversation  this is a great this link is al