's a commercial product as well  excellent thank you sean  so i'm going to try and synthesize uh  several questions here  because they're they there are a couple  of big groups that they fall into  some of them fall into at least one is  there's a series of questions  that's along the lines of why should i  learn  python one question are asked does the  data scientist need to know all the  languages  another questioner asks what's better in  r another questionnaire asks  what's better in python and one  questionnaire suggests  that in our ggplot and shiny are  particular strengths  of r that python users might want to  leverage so any thoughts there  yeah those are great questions uh i  think  what we've really come to appreciate is  that  kind of pitting r and python against one  another  just isn't really practical for data  scientists and data science teams  at the end of the day people are coming  from different backgrounds  some have learned r some have learned  python some have learned both  and so really the the conversation to  have  is how do you use r and python together  you know  that kind of love story idea as opposed  to  which languages is best both are going  to be around for the long term  uh and it is really going to depend on  kind of your context  there's a lot of domain specific  packages in both languages  so if you're working in the insurance  industry versus in the finance industry  you might find yourself  using your different packages for  different needs um  so so i know that's a little bit of  dodging the question  the key thing i think is is to not get  hung up  on you know should i only learn r or  should i only learn python  really both languages are going to be  needed to succeed  excellent thank you there's another  series of questions that are all around  configuration and versioning there's one  set of questions  that asks well i don't see this option  what version of our studio products do i  need  there's another question that asks does  this support python two  or three or both and then third a more  general set of questions around how is  installation and configuration and  versioning  of python uh handled yeah those are  really great questions so let me quickly  go back to our studio id uh  rstudio 1.2 is going to be the version  that  has uh this built-in support uh  so that's what you should look for and  you can see your version of our studio  and just help  about our studio 1.2 is what what you're  going to look for there  you'll also want to install the  reticulate package that's going to  unlock a lot of this capability  and if you do try to use like an r  markdown document with r and python  the ide can guide you towards installing  that package as well  uh so that's kind of the the jumping off  point  there's two topics around configuration  so one is how do i handle configuration  if i'm working with others and we talked  a lot in the webinar about how it's  helpful  to be running our studio on a  server-side environment  where it can work with you to have a  consistent environment for the team  so that when you get started either in a  jupiter notebook or  inside of our studio you have a kind of  shared playbook  that's a baseline environment whether  that's r or python  or the sets of packages required by both  uh so that would be my recommendation  especially if you're working with others  specific to python management uh  inside of our studio for those of you  that are combining r and python together  the reticulate uh package has a whole  bunch of functions that specify  what python environment to use so if  you're familiar with like virtual  environments or conda  you can create those and then tell r to  use them  there's also functions for creating  those things  so if you're more of an r user but you  know you need to install some python  packages to get things to work  you can take a look at those create and  install  functions so there's a lot of  flexibility there  there's a reticulate cheat sheet  available online that's going to walk  through  a lot of that stuff and then the one tip  i would give folks  is that the pi uh config  pi discover config so this function here  uh is really helpful in that it tells  you kind of  what a version of python has been  identified  is in it and is in use by the r session  uh kind of looking ahead one of the  things we're really excited about  working on this year  is extending the support for reticulate  so that you're able to do a bit more of  that environment management  uh from the rcu ide with a little bit of  help  you know interactively through the ui  so hopefully that answers some of the  questions there lou there's  a question on um  will this functionality be available in  our studio cloud  yeah excellent question so our studio  cloud  today does not have the the python  environments installed  um but if that's something that's  interesting to you we'd love to to chat  about  you know what types of projects you're  hoping to work on  for those that are completely unfamiliar  our studio cloud  is an environment for  kind of right now specifically geared at  teaching though it also can be useful  for for individual projects as well  uh what it essentially is is a version  of our studio  that we run so everything we were  talking about today  in the webinar was for teams that are  maintaining  their own infrastructure or running our  studio just locally on your desktop  but if you're an educator and you want  to teach students about  r and python it can be really annoying  to  have to spend the whole you know first  week of class just trying to get r and r  studio  and python and jupiter all installed and  so rstudio  cloud solves that problem for you that's  support for  uh what we call different workspaces so  you can set up a class  invite students to that class and then  they have an r studio environment  that's configured and ready to go and so  if you're interested in using python in  your courses  alongside of r and we'd love to hear  from you so that we can understand  what your requirements are on python  might be someone in the question box had  called us out for having python 2.7  in our environment and you're absolutely  right  python 2 isn't supported anymore but  practically it's still all around  we'd love to get your feedback  especially from educators  on what you're hoping to teach and what  that right environment might look like  for you  we got from one of the listeners who  like to know where they can find more  resources regarding use cases for data  scientists or devops and it admins  um perhaps you want to show  solutions.rtstudio.com real quick unless  you've got another suggestion on that  yeah absolutely so lou had mentioned  there's a lot of great resources  as part of this webinar that we'll be  sharing afterwards  um one of the the things i'll point you  to  is this site called rstudio solutions  it's available just at  solutions.rcu.com this is going to have  a bunch of those examples for what  workflows look like  for data science teams and this is based  on our interactions with more than a  thousand  commercial entities that are doing work  you know running both of these languages  in production  and it's also going to give some  pointers for the i.t or devops  side of the house who are trying to  answer questions like what should our  architecture look like  you know should we be doing things in  kubernetes should we do  doing things in the cloud should be  running things locally  so so all those questions are answered  here then of course we're happy to talk  with you as well  on the slides include that link  to set up a meeting if you want to  bounce ideas off of anyone on our team  so we have a few applica a few questions  around  deploying shiny apps onto connect and  how does that  how that works one asks whether or not  it's able to utilize a cluster behind  the scenes  another asks once it's published on  connect  who is going to continue management how  does it scale up  for more users to access and another one  asks whether it's possible to make that  available outside of firewall  yeah awesome questions really exciting  to hear about  you know people delivering those  applications into production that's  certainly  probably the top thing that our  customers are working on  is how to get shiny applications you  know off of your laptop or your  development environment  into somewhere that's more stable we can  answer those  questions specifically if folks have  them  um what i'll show you here connect is  basically a product that runs on a  server  it scales horizontally so if you you  are concerned about having more people  visiting your application  than a single server can handle you can  add multiple servers just like you would  with any other web application  and place them behind a load balancer  the deployment  happens in a variety of ways i showed  you push button publishing  rco connect also knows how to integrate  with git  so if you want to have a more get  centric workflow you can do that  once the content is deployed there's a  lot that happens  to help you manage kind of the ongoing  life cycle of that content  so we mentioned that you can look at the  logs uh we talked about  some of those scaling settings that'll  you know help  as you go to do that type of horizontal  scaling  and then there's also things like  default source versions  the ability to roll back and forwards in  time so there's just a lot  of options there at the end of the day  our goal what we're trying to do is help  bridge that gap that often exists  between data scientists who are creating  these amazing applications and then i t  operations  who know a ton about servers and about  best practices around things like  infrastructure as code but might be less  familiar  with r and python and so rco connect  acts as that bridge uh to serve a really  nice balance where data scienti